/// Flak Cannon. Cost 2, random-enemy Attack. Count the owner's Status
/// cards in every pile except Exhaust (`hits`), exhaust all of them,
/// then deal `base` damage `hits` times — each hit re-rolling a random
/// alive enemy. Mirrors C# `FlakCannon` (FlakCannon.cs:33-52):
/// `GetStatuses = AllCards.Where(Type==Status && Pile != Exhaust)`,
/// `statusCount = CalculatedHits`, `foreach Exhaust`, then
/// `DamageCmd.Attack(base).WithHitCount(statusCount)
/// .TargetingRandomOpponents`. The hit count is captured BEFORE the
/// exhaust, and the per-hit random target reads `combat_targets`
/// (`get_random_enemy`) — both matching C#.
///
/// Pile iteration order matters because it is the order the statuses
/// land in the exhaust pile (observable). C# `AllCards` walks
/// `AllPiles` in the fixed order Hand, Draw, Discard, Exhaust, Play
/// (`PlayerCombatState.cs:48`), and the filter drops Exhaust — so the
/// effective order is hand → draw → discard → play. (Bead
/// `sts2-rs-1t7oj`: was draw → hand → … which
/// flipped the relative exhaust order of two statuses split across
/// hand and draw.)
/// The Status cards Flak Cannon exhausts, in C# `AllPiles` order so the
/// resulting exhaust-pile order matches. `CalculatedHits` is this list's
/// length — the two share one scan so the count can never disagree with what
/// is actually exhausted.
fn flak_cannon_statuses(state: &CombatState) -> smallvec::SmallVec<[sts2_core::CardUid; 8]> {
    state
        .player
        .hand
        .iter()
        .chain(state.player.draw.iter())
        .chain(state.player.discard.iter())
        .chain(state.player.play.iter())
        .copied()
        .filter(|uid| state.cards[uid.0.get() as usize - 1].card_type == CardType::Status)
        .collect()
}

